The task was already done and committed before the previous attempt was interrupted. I re-ran every check on that commit and they all pass, so there was nothing left to change or commit.

What the commit (`19d5d3ce`) does: MCP stdio servers now start without your provider API keys by default, the same way hooks already did.
• One change point: mcp_stdio_options() in src/mcp_prefix.rs now adds .env_remove_all(subprocess_env::hook_scrub_vars()). Both server launches (the startup check and the real connect) go through that one function, and no other place starts a stdio server.
• No new rules: it reuses the hooks' switch and key list. Unset, 1 or a misspelled value removes the keys; 0 or false leaves them. GITHUB_TOKEN and GH_TOKEN are kept, and the keys are removed entirely, not set to empty strings.
• Cost to users: a server that relied on picking up a key from your environment will now fail its own calls. The fix is to give it the key in [mcp_servers.<name>] env = { KEY = "..." }, which still works because yoagent applies that map after removing the keys. Setting YOYO_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=0 also works. The model sees the same tools as before.

Checks re-run just now:
• cargo fmt -- --check and cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings pass.
• The new tests/mcp_env_scrub.rs passes 6 of 6. It runs the real binary against a stand-in "server" that writes out its environment, covering unset, 1, a misspelled value, 0, false, and the config env map. Each case also checks that a harmless test variable arrived, so a server that never started can't pass.
• The full cargo test gives 34 "test result: ok" lines and no failures.
• No NEUTERED markers are left in the code.
• The commit message records the deliberate-break check: removing the key-stripping made exactly the unset, 1 and misspelled cases fail by name, and restoring it brought all 6 back.

